1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
This is Task 2, Agile planning and the DevOps workflow.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Agile means we build in small steps and keep a list of work to do.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
I am listing the headings of the planning document.

4
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:24,000
It has user stories, a product backlog, a Kanban board, a sprint plan, the Definition of Done, and the DevOps lifecycle diagram.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
I will walk through each one.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:37,000
A user story is one sentence that says who wants what and why, in the form: as a guest, I want something, so that something.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Stories one to six are the six booking features.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Stories seven to nine are for the developer and the operator: automatic testing, one command deployment, and rollback.

9
00:00:49,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Writing needs as stories keeps every feature tied to a real person.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Acceptance criteria say exactly when a story is finished.

11
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:04,000
Here is story five, cancelling a booking.

12
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:11,000
A pending or confirmed booking becomes cancelled, the room becomes free, and you cannot cancel twice.

13
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,000
Notice that the last part names the automated tests that check it.

14
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
So the criteria are not opinions, they are tests.

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Now I prove story five live.

16
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:29,000
I run only the tests whose name contains cancel.

17
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Seven tests run and seven should pass, including cannot cancel twice and other user cannot cancel.

18
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:42,000
If these pass, the acceptance criteria of the cancellation story are met today.

19
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
The product backlog is the ordered list of work.

20
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:57,000
Our backlog items are HB one to HB fifteen, one for each of the fifteen course tasks, with the task number and a status.

21
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,000
All are done.

22
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Items like payments are not here, because they are out of scope.

23
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
I want to be honest about GitHub issues.

24
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
This command asks GitHub, live, how many issues the public repository has.

25
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The answer is zero.

26
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:27,000
The backlog exists in the document, and the issue and pull request templates are ready in the dot github folder, but the real GitHub issues have not been created yet.

27
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:31,000
That is the one remaining step for this task.

28
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:40,000
A Kanban board shows work moving through columns: backlog, to do, in progress, review and done.

29
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Each card moved from left to right.

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:50,000
A card reached review when its feature branch was ready to merge, and done when it met the Definition of Done.

31
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Payments and an admin screen stay in the backlog as future work.

32
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:02,000
A sprint is a short fixed period of work with a goal.

33
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:05,000
We planned three sprints in ten working days.

34
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:13,000
Sprint one is foundation, sprint two is automation with Jenkins and Docker, and sprint three is operations and release.

35
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:21,000
Each sprint ends with a review, a demo of what works, and a retrospective, what to keep and what to improve.

36
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The Definition of Done is the checklist that must be true before any item counts as finished.

37
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Code on a feature branch, automated tests, a clean linter, a green Jenkins pipeline, a merge into develop, updated documentation and no secrets.

38
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,000
This is how completion is determined, so nobody can say it is done when it is not.

39
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
This is the DevOps lifecycle, written as a diagram in text form.

40
00:03:55,000 --> 00:03:57,000
It is drawn automatically on GitHub.

41
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
Plan, code, build, test.

42
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
If any test fails we stop and fix, there is no deployment.

43
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:16,000
If everything is green we release, deploy, operate and monitor, and if there is a problem we roll back to the previous version, and then planning starts again.

44
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Finally, here is the same workflow in real code.

45
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:36,000
These are the stages in our Jenkinsfile, in order: checkout, install, static checks, tests, Selenium browser tests, package, build images, publish, deploy and health check.

46
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:41,000
A developer's code change passes through them one after another.

47
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:46,000
Only if the test stages pass does the pipeline reach deployment.

48
00:04:46,000 --> 00:04:51,000
That is how a task is traced from commit to operation.


